class Solution {
  public int weightedLinesEvenKeyCensus(int[][] links, int k) {
    int[][] a=links; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int n=a.length;int[]dist=new int[n];Arrays.fill(dist,Integer.MAX_VALUE);PriorityQueue<int[]>heap=new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(x->x[0]));if(n>0){dist[0]=0;heap.add(new int[]{0,0});}while(!heap.isEmpty()){int[]entry=heap.remove();int cost=entry[0],u=entry[1];if(cost!=dist[u])continue;for(int v=0;v<n;v++)if(a[u][v]>0&&cost+a[u][v]<dist[v]){dist[v]=cost+a[u][v];heap.add(new int[]{dist[v],v});}}for(int d:dist)out.add(d==Integer.MAX_VALUE?-1:d);}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)if(x%2==0)answer++;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

Each point in this graph is a place, and each one-way path has a travel cost. Start at point 0. Find the lowest total travel cost to each point. Put these costs in point order. The cost to point 0 is 0. Record -1 for any point you cannot reach. A graph with no points gives an empty result list.

Return the answer

Count the even numbers in the result list. An even number divides by 2 with no remainder. Count 0 and negative even numbers too. Return the count. An empty list gives 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(V^2 + E log V); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length; V is the number of graph points; E is the number of graph links. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

